      "note": "Root, label and gloss are correct. Pokorny 1168 groups exactly Nourai's material: Av. varəz- / varəza- 'work' (with 'npers. varz, barz Feldarbeit, Ackerbau'), Gk. érgon, órganon, Germanic *werka- (OE weorc, OHG werc). Watkins (AHD) lists work, bulwark, (boulevard), ergon (allergy, argon, energy, metallurgy, surgery, georgic) and organ/organon under werg-. Cheung 2007 (*u̯arz 'to do, work, till the land') notes NP varzidan and barz are not regular SW Persian forms (inherited SW Persian has d < PIr *ź, as in OP vard-); they come from a NW Iranian (Parthian-type) source. Weak spots on the chart: the gloss 'strong cow' for varzāv (it is the plough-ox), the label 'Middle German' for bolwerk (the French word comes from Middle Dutch, or possibly Middle High German, bolwerc), and the claim that argon and allergy came through French (they are modern coinages from Greek). The extraction matches the printed page (checked against site/pages/549.jpg). The page also has an empty, unlabelled box to the right of the Old Persian node that the JSON leaves out; it holds no word.",
